      "writtenOn": "2015-05-27T20:51:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As it says: you get a console that you can type into, similar to minicom or any other serial-port terminal program. Hitting ^C or ^D will exit. \n\nBut minicom, etc require the OS to recognize the USB device, create a pty, etc. Setting that up requires building and installing a custom kernel driver and tweaking udev rules (in extra/usb_serial), and then you get to fight with modemmanager over who should talk to it. That\u0027s a pain for all sorts of reasons, which is why I wrote this.",
      "parentUuid": "0d54fde2_30c388ba",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 23,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 23,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "23b6a380311f4b4c4e3b7c5bbe0b398ede552450",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ed56c9ec_15fe74aa",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 23,
      "author": {
        "id": 1112784
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-05-27T20:55:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just to be clear, the udev rules are not actually required, though they do ensure that ModemManager doesn\u0027t mess with the device.  And the kernel module isn\u0027t required either, you can just insmod the usbserial kernel module with vid:pid pair correctly set.  Also the changes required to add all google USB serial devices to the usbserial module are already upstream, and back ported to a number of kernels (all the way to 3.12... I believe).  So \"soon\" you won\u0027t even need to insmod with anything special.",
      "parentUuid": "ed56c9ec_72cd16e0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 23,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 23,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "23b6a380311f4b4c4e3b7c5bbe0b398ede552450",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
